---
/**
 * AI prompt footprint calculator
 *
 * Readers build their usage from rows of (model x output length x count per
 * day), which sum into one daily and annual carbon and water cost. The page
 * compares it with entered digital activities and fixed everyday references.
 * Location sets the electricity grid factor. All calculator inputs and chart selections are URL-shareable.
 *
 * Per-model energy / carbon / water estimates (including uncertainty ranges)
 * come from the EcoLogits library (v0.10), the engine behind the EcoLogits
 * calculator at huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator. All
 * other figures are documented in the methodology section at the bottom.
 */
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const pageTitle = 'AI carbon & water footprint calculator (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini)';
const ogTitle = 'What does your chatbot use actually cost?';
const pageDescription =
  'A free calculator: enter your daily ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini prompts to see the carbon and water footprint of your AI use, compared with your digital activities, everyday things, and bigger lifestyle changes.';
const pageUrl = 'https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint';
const ogImage = '/images/og/og-ai-prompt-footprint.png';
const datePublished = '2026-06-10';
const dateModified = '2026-06-10';
const keywords = [
  'AI carbon footprint',
  'AI water footprint',
  'ChatGPT carbon footprint',
  'ChatGPT water usage',
  'AI energy consumption',
  'carbon footprint calculator',
  'water footprint calculator',
  'environmental impact of AI',
  'EcoLogits',
  'Claude',
  'Gemini',
];

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: pageTitle,
    description: pageDescription,
    url: pageUrl,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    datePublished,
    dateModified,
    keywords: keywords.join(', '),
    primaryImageOfPage: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://andymasley.com' + ogImage, width: 1200, height: 630 },
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com' },
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
    about: [
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Artificial intelligence' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Carbon footprint' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Water footprint' },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'AI prompt footprint calculator',
    url: pageUrl,
    applicationCategory: 'UtilityApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any (web browser)',
    browserRequirements: 'Requires JavaScript',
    description: pageDescription,
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How much carbon does one AI prompt produce?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'It depends on the model and how long the reply is. Using EcoLogits estimates, a typical chatbot reply ranges from under 0.02 g CO2e on a small model to a few grams on a large one, with reasoning models and long outputs much higher. For most people a full day of AI use is a tiny fraction of one percent of their daily carbon footprint.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Does AI use a lot of water?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'A single text prompt consumes only a few milliliters to a few liters of water (data-center cooling plus power generation). Even heavy daily use is far less than the embodied water in everyday food and goods, such as a cup of coffee (about 140 liters) or a beef burger (about 1,700 liters).',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How does AI use compare to driving, flying, or diet?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'A year of typical AI use is dwarfed by a single transatlantic flight, a year of driving, or your diet. Cutting your AI use saves far less carbon than living car-free, switching to clean electricity, or eating less meat.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Does this include the cost of training AI models?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'No. Training is excluded because it is uncertain and, divided across all the users of a model, adds very little to an individual person’s footprint.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Visuals', item: 'https://andymasley.com/visuals' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: pageTitle, item: pageUrl },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  canonicalUrl={pageUrl}
  ogTitle={ogTitle}
  ogImage={ogImage}
  ogImageAlt="Andy Masley's AI carbon and water footprint calculator"
  keywords={keywords}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="aipf" id="aipf">
    <header class="aipf-header">
      <h1 class="aipf-title">What your chatbot use actually costs</h1>
      <p class="aipf-dek">
        Enter an average day of AI use and this tool adds it up into one carbon and water cost, set
        against your digital activities and everyday things. Numbers for text AI's energy and water come from
        <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>,
        an open-source estimator of the energy, carbon, and water behind each AI model. The Claude
        numbers are the shakiest here: Anthropic hasn't said how Claude is built, so EcoLogits has
        to guess, and its guess might run low
        (<a href="#methodology" onclick="document.getElementById('methodology').open=true">more in the methodology</a>). For carbon I
        use the average grid intensity of the region you pick for text AI (not EcoLogits' single whole-world
        average) plus the hardware's embodied emissions; everything else is sourced in the methodology
        below. Training is excluded as too uncertain, but split across all users it
        <a href="https://blog.andymasley.com/p/training-ai-models-doesnt-emit-that" target="_blank" rel="noopener">adds little to your personal footprint</a>.
        For a fuller carbon picture, and why grid-level changes swamp everything else, see
        <a href="https://www.andymasley.com/visuals/carbon-footprint/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">my visual here</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="aipf-toolbar-top">
      <div class="aipf-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Footprint type">
        <span class="aipf-toggle-label">Show</span>
        <div class="aipf-showing">
          <button type="button" class="aipf-metric is-active" data-metric="carbon">💨 Carbon</button>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-metric" data-metric="water">💧 Water</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="aipf-report-btn" id="aipf-report"
        title="Generate a report with citations of exactly how my AI use as entered here contributes to my carbon emissions.">
        Generate a personalized cited report of my AI emissions
      </button>
    </div>

    <!-- Tool: inputs on the left, the answer on the right -->
    <div class="aipf-tool">
      <div class="aipf-in" aria-label="Your daily AI usage">
        <div class="aipf-in-head"><h2>Your AI use</h2><span class="aipf-in-sub">daily use &amp; project sessions</span></div>

        <div class="aipf-usage-section" aria-label="Chatbot and text prompts">
          <h3 class="aipf-usage-h">Chatbot &amp; text</h3>
          <div class="aipf-rows-head" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>Model</span><span>Typical output</span><span>Prompts/day</span><span>Retries</span><span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="aipf-rows" id="aipf-rows"></div>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-addrow" id="aipf-addrow">+ Add another</button>
          <p class="aipf-hint">"Typical output" is how long each reply is.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="aipf-usage-section" aria-label="Coding agent project sessions">
          <h3 class="aipf-usage-h">Coding agent — project sessions</h3>
          <div class="aipf-rows-head aipf-rows-head--code" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>Model</span><span>Lines of code</span><span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="aipf-rows" id="aipf-rows-code"></div>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-addrow" id="aipf-addrow-code">+ Add a session</button>
          <p class="aipf-project-total" id="aipf-project-total" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">A "coding / agent session" means one full run of an AI coding assistant that writes and edits code across many steps — you tell it roughly how many lines of code the session produced, and it estimates the rest. Lines convert to an estimated token count (<code>lines × 10 ÷ 15%</code>, using our rough assumptions of 10 tokens per line and a 15% code fraction, not measured universal ratios), then that token count is costed at the same per-token energy/carbon/water rate as the record retained from Masley’s calculator for the EcoLogits 100,000-token "assist application development" benchmark for the chosen model. This is a rough, linearly-scaled estimate, not a separate study of sessions at every length — and it does not use Couch's separate self-reported energy method. For scale: about 1,500 lines matches that fixed 100,000-token EcoLogits benchmark exactly (a fixed content-length benchmark, not typical-session data), and about 8,900 lines matches one developer's self-logged ~592,000-token session (<a href="https://simonpcouch.com/blog/2026-01-20-cc-impact/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Couch</a>, n=1) — one person's data, not a representative average.</p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Each session is a one-time project cost, not a recurring daily use — that is why this section has no "per day" framing. The project total above sums every session and stays separate from your combined AI-use total and the day/year figures elsewhere on this page.</p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Sessions have no retry multiplier, unlike the entries below. A session's lines-of-code figure already describes one full run, and re-running an entire agent session rarely reproduces the same amount of code — so a repeated run is better represented as its own separate session, with its own line count, than as a flat multiple of this one.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="aipf-usage-section" aria-label="Image and video generation">
          <h3 class="aipf-usage-h">Media generation</h3>
          <h4 class="aipf-usage-subh">Image generation</h4>
          <div class="aipf-rows-head aipf-rows-head--image" aria-hidden="true"><span>Images/day</span><span>Retries</span><span></span></div>
          <div class="aipf-rows" id="aipf-rows-image"></div>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-addrow" id="aipf-addrow-image">+ Add images</button>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Images use <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16863" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luccioni et al.'s published average</a> of 2.907 Wh per image across eight open Stable Diffusion models. This is a proxy for commercial tools, not a measurement of every provider. We use this average as a single estimate; the source's per-model extremes are not an uncertainty range around it. Carbon and water are our own calculations from energy, not published average carbon or measured water figures.</p>
          <h4 class="aipf-usage-subh">Video generation</h4>
          <div class="aipf-rows-head aipf-rows-head--video" aria-hidden="true"><span>Clips/day</span><span>Seconds/clip</span><span>Retries</span><span></span></div>
          <div class="aipf-rows" id="aipf-rows-video"></div>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-addrow" id="aipf-addrow-video">+ Add video clips</button>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Video uses a <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04553" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Veo 3 estimate</a> of 19.8–43.4 Wh (mean 30.8 Wh) for an 8-second, 720p clip. It is inferred from API response times and assumed hardware, not direct energy measurement; the project's source assessment records the paper as awaiting peer review. We multiply by <a href="https://datacenters.google/efficiency/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's 1.09 facility-overhead factor</a> as a separate assumption. Other providers may differ.</p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">720p is fixed. Doubling seconds doubles the estimate: that linear scaling is our assumption, not a finding about other clip lengths. Video energy and carbon show a range; water uses the source's mean energy and is a single derived estimate.</p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Media carbon uses your selected location's electricity factor, without a hardware-emissions estimate. Media water uses <a href="https://www.nlr.gov/docs/fy04osti/33905.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NREL’s historical U.S. thermoelectric factor of 0.47 gallons per kWh</a> for electricity-generation cooling. It is derived, not measured, and does not estimate on-site data-center cooling. Counts are daily; retries multiply each entry once.</p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Copy link and the cited report include text, media, coding sessions, digital-life activities, router settings, and chart selections.</p>
        </div>

        <p class="aipf-running" id="aipf-running" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <section class="aipf-pie-section" aria-labelledby="aipf-pie-heading">
          <h3 class="aipf-usage-h" id="aipf-pie-heading">What makes up your footprint?</h3>
          <p class="aipf-hint">One day of entered AI use, digital activities, and the always-on router. Coding sessions count once only when you select them in the project builder.</p>
          <div class="aipf-pie-controls">
            <label for="aipf-pie-metric">Show <select id="aipf-pie-metric"><option value="carbon">Carbon</option><option value="water">Water</option></select></label>
            <label for="aipf-pie-bound">Estimate <select id="aipf-pie-bound"><option value="low">Lower</option><option value="high">Upper</option></select></label>
          </div>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Lower/upper uses the corresponding end of each available range; single estimates stay the same. These are illustrative scenarios, not confidence limits for the total. Source coverage differs across activities; this is not a complete personal footprint.</p>
          <p id="aipf-pie-total" class="aipf-pie-total" aria-live="polite"></p>
          <div class="aipf-pie-disc" id="aipf-pie-disc" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="aipf-pie-table-wrap"><table class="aipf-pie-table">
            <caption id="aipf-pie-caption">One-day breakdown</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Contribution</th><th scope="col">Estimate shown</th><th scope="col">Share</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody id="aipf-pie-values"></tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Zero contributions are omitted. Percentages may differ slightly from 100% after rounding. Selections affect only this chart, not daily AI totals or project totals. Chart selections are saved in shared links and included in the cited report.</p>
        </section>
        <p class="aipf-hint">Each chatbot/text, image, or video row's "×" field counts retries or regenerations of that same prompt. It defaults to 1 (the original output only) — no research gives a typical retry rate, so any higher number is your own estimate of your behavior, not a study finding.</p>
        <p class="aipf-tools">
          <button type="button" class="aipf-link" id="aipf-reset">Reset</button>
          <span class="aipf-tools-sep">·</span>
          <button type="button" class="aipf-link" id="aipf-share">Copy link</button>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="aipf-out" aria-label="How it compares">
        <div class="aipf-out-head">
          <label class="aipf-grid-setting" for="aipf-loc">Electricity grid
            <select id="aipf-loc" aria-label="Electricity grid location"></select>
          </label>
        </div>
        <section class="aipf-digital" aria-labelledby="aipf-digital-title">
          <h2 class="aipf-h2" id="aipf-digital-title">Your digital life</h2>
          <p class="aipf-sub">Enter daily hours for each activity (0–24; decimals are welcome). These comparisons stay separate from your AI use. Each activity is independent, so overlapping hours are allowed.</p>
          <div class="aipf-activities" id="aipf-activities"></div>
          <div class="aipf-project-total" id="aipf-digital-total" aria-live="polite"></div>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Yearly estimates assume the same daily hours for 365 days. Gaming and any total that includes it show a low–high range, not an average. On comparison bars, a patterned end shows that range. The combined activity total excludes AI, coding projects, and the router.</p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">These sources cover different parts of each activity, so this sum is a rough comparison, not a complete digital footprint. All activity water is derived from electricity using <a href="https://www.nlr.gov/docs/fy04osti/33905.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NREL’s historical U.S. thermoelectric factor of 0.47 gal/kWh</a>; it represents electricity-generation cooling, not measured water use or on-site data-center cooling.</p>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Activity hours are included in Copy link and the cited report.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="aipf-digital" aria-labelledby="aipf-router-title">
          <h2 class="aipf-h2" id="aipf-router-title">Home Wi-Fi / router</h2>
          <p class="aipf-sub">An always-on baseline: 24 hours a day, regardless of your activity hours. This is a separate infrastructure comparison, outside both AI-use and digital-activity totals.</p>
          <div class="aipf-rowwrap" id="aipf-router-control"></div>
          <p class="aipf-hint" id="aipf-router-guide"><strong>Rough reference range: 5–20 W.</strong> Enter your router’s estimated average power above, or use a measured value if available. At 24 hours a day, this range uses 0.12–0.48 kWh per day. Watts describe power draw, not daily energy use; your equipment may fall outside this range.</p>
          <div class="aipf-activity-results" id="aipf-router-results" aria-live="polite"></div>
          <details class="aipf-activity-source">
            <summary>Source and limitations</summary>
            <p class="aipf-hint">The 5–20 W guide is a user-selected estimate informed by their research and the range in <a href="https://futuretechinsights.io/smart-home/broadband-router-electricity-cost-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Future Tech Insights</a>. That article does not document wattage measurements and contains cost-calculation errors; this is a rough input guide, not a verified average or a required input limit. Calculations use your entered wattage.</p>
            <p class="aipf-hint">The adjustable 6.5 W default is a chosen example, not a measured average or a verified component-allowance total. Enter an estimate for your own router or gateway; its power draw may be higher or lower.</p>
            <p class="aipf-hint">Electricity is watts × 24 ÷ 1,000 kWh per day; yearly figures multiply by 365. Carbon is derived using your selected electricity grid. Water is derived using <a href="https://www.nlr.gov/docs/fy04osti/33905.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NREL’s historical U.S. thermoelectric factor of 0.47 gallons per kWh</a> for electricity-generation cooling, not measured router water use. This is the assumed full-day electricity cost, not an extra cost caused by streaming or other individual activities.</p>
          </details>
          <p class="aipf-hint">Router wattage is included in Copy link and the cited report.</p>
        </section>
        <div class="aipf-verdict" id="aipf-headline" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <p class="aipf-tools aipf-tools--summary">
          <button type="button" class="aipf-link" id="aipf-share-summary">Copy my summary</button>
        </p>
        <div class="aipf-mini">
          <p class="aipf-chart-title">A day of your AI use vs. everyday things</p>
          <p class="aipf-mini-h" id="aipf-daily-sub"></p>
          <div class="aipf-bars aipf-bars--compact" id="aipf-daily-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="aipf-fine" id="aipf-grid-note"></p>
        <p class="aipf-fine">Includes text, image, and video generation with entered retries. Excludes training; coding sessions remain a separate project total.</p>
        <p class="aipf-fine">*A note on the Claude models: Anthropic has never said how Claude is built, so EcoLogits has to guess. Its guess is that Claude Opus and Sonnet only use a fraction of their parameters at a time. Some people who follow this closely think Claude actually uses all of them on every token, and if that's right, the Claude numbers here are too low. <a href="#methodology" onclick="document.getElementById('methodology').open=true">More in the methodology below</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Below: a year, split into emissions added vs cuts you could make -->
    <div class="aipf-below" aria-label="In a year, ways you add">
      <h2 class="aipf-h2" id="aipf-add-title">In a year, ways you add emissions</h2>
      <p class="aipf-sub" id="aipf-add-sub"></p>
      <div class="aipf-bars" id="aipf-add-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="aipf-below" aria-label="In a year, ways you can cut">
      <h2 class="aipf-h2" id="aipf-cut-title">In a year, ways you can cut emissions</h2>
      <p class="aipf-sub" id="aipf-cut-sub"></p>
      <div class="aipf-bars" id="aipf-cut-bars" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="aipf-below" aria-label="What if your whole company did this">
      <h2 class="aipf-h2">What if your whole company did this?</h2>
      <p class="aipf-sub">
        How many people work at your company?
        <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" class="aipf-headcount" id="aipf-headcount" min="1" max="1000000" step="1" value="300" aria-label="Number of employees" />
      </p>
      <p class="aipf-sub" id="aipf-company-sub" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>

    <details class="aipf-method" id="methodology">
      <summary>How these numbers are made</summary>
      <div class="aipf-method-body">
        <p><strong>Estimates retained from Andy Masley.</strong> This project adapts <a href="https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint-source.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Masley’s original calculator source</a>. The following stored estimates were retained unchanged. Their presence in the original code is verified; this project has not independently reproduced them from the underlying studies.</p>
        <p><strong>AI model records:</strong> all 72 records across nine models and eight output sizes, including energy, embodied hardware carbon, water, and stored low/high estimates. Masley attributes these to EcoLogits. Text calculations use these records, and our coding-project conversion scales a retained record. The snapshot’s version, water assumptions, and statistical interpretation remain subject to verification.</p>
        <p><strong>Retained carbon comparisons:</strong> coffee, PS5, microwave, driving, shower, dishwasher, dryer, book printing; T-shirt, jeans, smartphone, sofa, bicycle, laptop, TV, flights and car manufacture. Retained savings cover LEDs, recycling, hang-drying, hybrid/electric cars, green electricity, home retrofit, green heating, and living car-free.</p>
        <p><strong>Retained water comparisons:</strong> book printing, home electricity, T-shirt, jeans, smartphone, buying five fewer cotton garments, native planting, and leaving a lawn unwatered. These inherited values retain their approximation and accounting-boundary limitations. Attribution establishes provenance, not independent validation or like-for-like comparability.</p>
        <p><strong>Project additions and revisions:</strong> grid factors now use OWID/Ember’s 2025 data. Media generation, coding line-count conversion, digital-life activities, router estimates, workplace and air-conditioning comparisons, and bottle/rose/tobacco water comparisons were added or changed here. Their sources and assumptions are described separately in this methodology and beside the inputs.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Per-model AI estimates.</strong> The energy, carbon, and water numbers for each
          prompt come from the open-source <a href="https://ecologits.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>
          library (v0.10), the same code that runs the
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits calculator</a>.
          The model list is current as of June 2026. EcoLogits estimates electricity from how big
          each model is (really, how many parameters it uses on each token) and how long the reply
          is, then adds the cost of manufacturing the hardware. The <a href="https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint-source.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">original Masley source</a> describes the stored low and high ends as EcoLogits’ 95% confidence interval. We have not independently verified that statistical interpretation for these records; the displayed endpoints are retained source estimates, not a verified confidence interval. One of its guesses matters a lot
          here: Anthropic has never said how Claude is built, and
          <a href="https://github.com/genai-impact/ecologits/blob/main/ecologits/data/models.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits' model data</a>
          assumes Claude Opus and Sonnet are mixture-of-experts models, which would mean they only
          use a fraction of their parameters on any given token (it marks every Claude entry
          "model-arch-not-released"). People who track this disagree. Some think the clues we do
          have, like Claude's speed and pricing, point to dense models that use every parameter
          every time. Others think Claude is a very large mixture-of-experts. If the dense camp is
          right, the Claude numbers on this page are probably too low. The water numbers count
          consumption, not withdrawal: water that is actually gone, evaporated cooling the data
          center or generating its electricity, not water taken in and then returned. Accounting boundaries differ: the derived electricity-water estimates omit on-site cooling, while some legacy product comparisons use broader water footprints. They are approximate context, not interchangeable measurements.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Carbon basis.</strong>
          <a href="https://ecologits.ai/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a> gives each
          prompt's electricity use and its embodied (hardware-manufacturing) emissions separately. We
          keep EcoLogits' embodied figure as-is and cost the electricity on the grid where you live,
          using carbon intensities from
          <a href="https://github.com/owid/energy-data/blob/master/owid-energy-data.csv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ember data via OWID (2025 observations)</a>
          and <a href="https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/carbon-intensity-electricity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Our World in Data</a>:
          384.40 (US), 209.897 (EU, 27 countries), 217.41 (UK), 525.34 (China), 670.13 (India), and 458.29 (world)
          g CO<sub>2</sub>e/kWh. These are 2025 lifecycle electricity-generation intensities from the <a href="https://github.com/owid/energy-data/blob/master/owid-energy-codebook.csv" target="_blank" rel="noopener">carbon_intensity_elec field</a>, retrieved September 26, 2026. Water is left on EcoLogits' global basis.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Text outputs and coding projects.</strong> Text rows use the selected EcoLogits output-size record. Daily text, image, and video totals multiply by 365 for the yearly scenario; this assumes the same use every day, not a prediction. Coding sessions remain whole-project values and are never annualized.</p>
        <p><strong>Coding conversion — calculator assumptions.</strong> We assume 10 tokens per line and a 15% code fraction: estimated tokens = lines × 10 ÷ 0.15. These are rough calculator assumptions, not measured universal ratios. Each model’s 100,000-token agent record retained from Masley’s calculator is divided by 100,000 and scaled to that estimate. Carbon adds the record's embodied hardware emissions to electricity × selected grid; water retains the record's EcoLogits basis. The 1,500-line anchor is our conversion of that fixed benchmark, not typical-session data. The approximately 8,900-line anchor converts <a href="https://simonpcouch.com/blog/2026-01-20-cc-impact/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Couch's 592,000-token median session</a>, from one person's logs (n=1); his separate energy method is not used.</p>
        <p><strong>Retries and regenerations.</strong> A multiplier of 1 counts the original generation once. Higher values are your estimate of total generations, including the original; we have no research-backed average retry count. Each text, image, or video entry is multiplied once before totals. Coding sessions have no multiplier: enter another session with its own line count for another run.</p>
        <p><strong>Ranges and comparison scope.</strong> Text uses the stored EcoLogits low/high estimates; video uses its source's inferred range; gaming spans console and desktop-PC assumptions. These are different kinds of uncertainty. Summed endpoints and the pie's lower/upper scenarios are not a statistical confidence interval for the combined total. Image, coding, streaming, video-call, social-media, and router estimates are points, not claims of certainty. The whole-project comparison includes all coding sessions once; pie selections include only chosen sessions once for the illustrated day. Neither changes daily or yearly AI totals.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Image and video generation.</strong> Images use the published 2.907 Wh average from
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16863" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luccioni et al.</a> as an open-model proxy.
          Video uses the indirectly inferred 8-second/720p Veo 3 range and mean from
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04553" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lights, Camera, Carbon</a>,
          with a separate 1.09 facility-overhead assumption from
          <a href="https://datacenters.google/efficiency/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google</a>.
          Duration scales linearly by our assumption. Media carbon is energy times the selected grid factor;
          water is energy times <a href="https://www.nlr.gov/docs/fy04osti/33905.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NREL’s historical U.S. thermoelectric factor of 0.47 gal/kWh</a>.
          Both are derived, excluding embodied hardware carbon and on-site cooling water. Image estimates
          are single points; video energy/carbon have a range and video water uses mean energy.
          See Media generation for proxy and evidence limitations.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Digital-life activities.</strong> Daily hours multiply each activity's energy factor;
          yearly values multiply the daily result by 365. Streaming retains IEA's published carbon figure.
          Video-call, gaming, and social-media carbon use the selected grid factor. Water uses the NREL
          electricity factor. Gaming's console-to-PC endpoints are summed separately into the combined
          activity range; no midpoint is invented. These activities never enter AI-use totals. <a href="#aipf-digital-title">Each activity's source and limitations</a> are available above.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Home Wi-Fi / router.</strong> The separate always-on baseline uses watts × 24 hours
          per day, then the selected electricity-grid carbon factor and NREL water factor. Annual values
          multiply by 365. It stays outside AI and activity totals and never scales with activity hours.
          <a href="#aipf-router-title">The router section</a> explains the chosen 6.5 W example and limitations.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Lifestyle cuts (the yearly chart).</strong> The "saved" figures come from the
          <a href="https://www.founderspledge.com/research/climate-and-lifestyle-report" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Founders Pledge Climate &amp; Lifestyle report</a>,
          drawing on
          <a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa7541" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wynes &amp; Nicholas (2017)</a>
          (living car-free 2.4 t, avoiding a transatlantic flight 1.6 t, hang-drying, recycling, LED
          bulbs) and
          <a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/ab8589" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ivanova et al. (2020)</a>
          (green electricity, heat-pump heating, a home retrofit, electric and hybrid cars).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ways you add emissions (the yearly chart).</strong>
          Manufacturing a new car ≈ 6 t CO<sub>2</sub>e
          (<a href="https://howbadarebananas.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Berners-Lee, How Bad Are Bananas?</a>);
          a <a href="https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2018-02/documents/lca_tv.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">new flat-screen TV ≈ 350 kg</a>;
          a <a href="https://devera.ai/benchmarks/carbon-footprint-of-a-laptop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">new laptop ≈ 250 kg</a>;
          a <a href="https://howbadarebananas.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">new bicycle ≈ 100 kg and a new sofa ≈ 90 kg</a>.
          Flights, per passenger
          (<a href="https://co2.myclimate.org/en/flight_calculators/new" target="_blank" rel="noopener">myclimate</a>,
          with a transatlantic round trip ≈ 1.6 t per
          <a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa7541" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wynes &amp; Nicholas</a>):
          a short-haul round trip ≈ 250 kg, a round-trip US
          cross-country flight ≈ 1 t, and a transatlantic round trip ≈ 1.6 t. A year of average US
          driving (~12,000 mi) ≈ 4.8 t at ~0.40 kg/mile
          (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/greenvehicles/greenhouse-gas-emissions-typical-passenger-vehicle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EPA</a>).
          a <a href="https://theconversation.com/coffee-heres-the-carbon-cost-of-your-daily-cup-and-how-to-make-it-climate-friendly-152629" target="_blank" rel="noopener">year of daily coffee ≈ 77 kg</a>;
          a <a href="https://howbadarebananas.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cotton T-shirt ≈ 7 kg</a>;
          a new smartphone ≈ 70 kg (<a href="https://www.apple.com/environment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apple</a>);
          and a pair of jeans ≈ 33 kg, retained from <a href="https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint-source.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Masley’s calculator</a> (whole-life estimate, including consumer care).
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Everyday things.</strong> A cup of coffee: ~0.21 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e and
          <a href="https://www.weforum.org/stories/2019/03/hidden-water-in-your-cup-of-coffee/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">140 L of water</a>.
          An hour on a <a href="https://www.playstation.com/en-gb/legal/ecodesign/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PS5 (~200 W)</a>;
          3 minutes in a <a href="https://www.siliconvalleypower.com/residents/save-energy/appliance-energy-use-chart" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1,200 W microwave</a>;
          a mile in a gas car (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/greenvehicles/greenhouse-gas-emissions-typical-passenger-vehicle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EPA, ~0.40 kg</a>);
          a 10-minute hot shower (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/watersense/showerheads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EPA WaterSense: ≤2 gallons/minute for labeled showerheads</a>, plus water heating);
          a <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/products/dishwashers/key_product_criteria" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dishwasher load (ENERGY STAR standard-size limit: ≤3.2 gal/cycle)</a>;
          a <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/products/clothes_dryers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dryer load (~3 kWh)</a>;
          <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1530-9290.2011.00414.x" target="_blank" rel="noopener">printing a 400-page book (~2.7 kg, Wells 2012)</a>
          with <a href="https://waterfootprint.org/resources/Report46-WaterFootprintPaper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">paper's water footprint</a>;
          a <a href="https://www.apple.com/environment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">new smartphone (~70 kg, Apple)</a>
          needing <a href="https://www.compareandrecycle.co.uk/blog/world-water-week-how-much-water-does-your-smartphone-use" target="_blank" rel="noopener">~12,800 L</a>;
          jeans: 33 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e and 1,321 US gallons (about 5,000 L) in the displayed comparison bars, retained from <a href="https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint-source.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Masley’s calculator source</a>. The water estimate’s blue-water boundary has not been independently verified here.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Fixed everyday comparisons — calculator assumptions.</strong> These reference bars retain rounded scenario estimates from the original calculator, using the linked sources as context. They do not change with the selected grid or activity inputs. Legacy electricity-based examples use an assumed US grid factor of 0.38 kg/kWh; the revised AC example uses the fixed 2025 US value of 0.38440 kg/kWh; coffee and product figures use broader life-cycle estimates. Shower heating, appliance loads, commute water, and workplace examples are calculator scenarios, not measured personal use. Treat these as approximate scale comparisons, not like-for-like measurements.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Calculations scaled from Masley.</strong> Our 20-page report comparison scales the book estimates in <a href="https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint-source.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Masley’s source</a>: carbon = 2.7 kg × 20/400 pages = 0.135 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e; water = 5 US gallons × 20/300 pages ≈ 0.33 gallon. The original carbon and water examples use different book lengths; scaling assumes proportional impact per page and retains their source limitations. Our 10-mile commute carbon scales Masley’s 0.40 kg/mile reference: 10 × 0.40 = 4 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e. Coding-project estimates scale Masley’s retained model records using our line-to-token assumptions. Annual and company scenarios further multiply the applicable daily totals by 365 and the entered headcount.</p>
        <p><strong>Air conditioning — chosen 2 kW example.</strong> We use the 2,000 W central-air-conditioner example from <a href="https://www.integrityacservice.com/about/blog/how-much-power-does-an-air-conditioner-really-use/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Integrity Services’ Example Calculation</a>. One hour of continuous operation uses 2 kWh. Our derived carbon is 2 × 0.38440 = 0.7688 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e, using the fixed 2025 US grid factor cited above. Electricity-generation water is 2 × 0.47 = 0.94 US gallon using the historical NREL factor cited below. This whole-unit example is not a measured office average or a per-person allocation; thermostat cycling and equipment vary. The comparison remains one hour, so the article’s eight-hour day, monthly duration, and electricity price do not enter these carbon/water calculations.</p>
        <p><strong>Commute water — back-of-the-envelope estimate.</strong> The fixed 10-mile round-trip commute uses an illustrative 0.15 US gallon water estimate. This is a calculator assumption, not a measured or independently verified research result. Its original fuel-use and water-intensity inputs are undocumented, so the derivation and accounting boundary cannot be verified. It stays fixed when the selected electricity grid changes.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Water comparisons (blue water).</strong> The water charts use the
          blue-water estimates where available, alongside retained references with broader or unverified accounting boundaries. These are not uniformly blue-water-only comparisons. Pacific Institute estimates two liters of production water beyond the contents for each liter of bottled water (three liters including the contents)
          (<a href="https://pacinst.org/wp-content/uploads/2013/02/bottled_water_and_energy3.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pacific Institute</a>).
          A cigarette's water footprint is about 3.7 L across its whole supply chain
          (<a href="https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.est.8b01533" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Environmental Science &amp; Technology, 2018</a>;
          left on a total, not blue-only, basis since tobacco's green/blue split isn't separately
          published). An imported cut rose consumes about 4 L of blue irrigation water per stem on
          Kenya's flower farms
          (<a href="https://www.waterfootprint.org/resources/Report45-WaterFootprint-Flowers-Kenya.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Water Footprint Network, Report 45</a>).
          A cotton T-shirt (~1,500 L of its 2,700 L total is blue), a pair of
          jeans (<a href="https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint-source.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Masley’s retained ~5,000 L estimate</a>, water boundary unverified), and a
          <a href="https://www.compareandrecycle.co.uk/blog/world-water-week-how-much-water-does-your-smartphone-use" target="_blank" rel="noopener">new smartphone</a>
          round out the manufactured goods. The savings come from cutting
          irrigated products (a few fewer cotton clothes) and outdoor watering: a typical lawn
          drinks roughly 20,000 gal of freshwater a year, so letting it go unwatered or replacing it
          with native plants saves most of that
          (<a href="https://www.epa.gov/watersense/landscaping-tips" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EPA WaterSense</a>).
          Energy and manufactured goods have a blue-water cost too: thermoelectric power plants
          are modeled here using a historical U.S. thermoelectric consumption factor of
          <a href="https://www.nlr.gov/docs/fy04osti/33905.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0.47 gal of freshwater per kWh</a>
          (NREL 2003, using 1995 data; cooling consumption only, not a current local-grid measurement), so our chosen hour of central air conditioning (2 kWh) consumes
          0.94 gal and a day of the
          <a href="https://www.eia.gov/tools/faqs/faq.php?id=97" target="_blank" rel="noopener">average US home's electricity (~29 kWh)</a>
          about 14 gal — the very same power-plant cooling water that makes up much of AI's footprint.
          Printing a 300-page book uses on the order of ~5 gal of process freshwater
          (<a href="https://waterfootprint.org/resources/Report46-WaterFootprintPaper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">paper's water footprint</a>
          is mostly rain on trees, with the blue mill water lower since most is returned to rivers).
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

